WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.669
-Our first guest is
a two-time Golden Globe winner,

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:06.539
and one of the most successful,
beloved movie stars on the
planet.

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.341
He's written this
brand-new novel called

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.110
"Memoirs and Misinformation,"
which is available now.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.345
And it's awesome.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.846
He's one of my
favorite humans ever.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:16.549
Here is<i> New York Times</i>
best-selling author,

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:19.318
the one, the only,
Jim Carrey, everyone!

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:21.888 line:0%
-Brother.
-Oh-ho! [ Laughs ]

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:25.057 line:0%
-Oh, man! Supercuts.
-Come on.

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:28.627 line:0%
-Supercuts is
the answer for you, buddy.

00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:33.566 line:0%
Supercuts and hazmat suits.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:36.035 line:0%
With garden shears from
a great distance.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:37.137 line:0%
-State of the art.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:38.637 line:0%
It's the best wig
you can get right now.

00:00:38.637 --> 00:00:40.540 line:0%
-I love it. I love it.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.641 line:0%
You look like you're in
the middle of

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:46.178 line:0%
a bar-room brawl in slow motion.

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.180 line:0%
-Which screams
midlife crisis more?

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.184 line:0%
The blazer with the shirt
or the messy hair?

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.186 line:0%
-Or the fact that
I'm free-balling right now?

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:56.488 line:0%
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:56.488 --> 00:00:59.159 line:0%
Holy crap.
-♪ Free ballin' ♪

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:01.061 line:0%
That's what that song
is about, man.

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:03.262 line:0%
-Are you kidding me, man?
I'm a big fan.

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:05.098 line:0%
I know all the hits, man.
-Yeah.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.332 line:0%
-No, this is true.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.268 line:0%
You are a<i> New York Times</i>
best-selling novelist.

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:12.271 line:0%
-What an incredible thing.
What a state of affairs.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:14.073 line:0%
-How does it feel?
-My goodness.

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:17.911 line:0%
It's a good sign for me,
a terrible sign for the planet.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:21.113 line:0%
-It is -- Does it --
Do you feel different?

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:22.248 line:0%
Are you walking different?

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.418 line:0%
-Well, my co-writer and me,
Dana Vachon,

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:27.720 line:0%
are literally children
running around

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.890 line:0%
right now like chickens
with our heads cut off.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.325 line:0%
We're very excited about it.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:35.895 line:0%
I mean, my gosh,
it's a dream come true.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.332 line:0%
-I will say, at first when
I got this, I go,

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.666 line:0%
"Oh, my goodness.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:41.934 line:0%
'Memoirs and Misinformation.'"

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:46.072 line:0%
I go, "It's just a celebrity
autobiographical memoir,

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.740 line:0%
and I can't wait to hear
the stories."

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.109 line:0%
And it is sort of that.
-Yes.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.844 line:0%
It gets to some real things.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:53.546 line:0%
There are real feelings in it
and everything,

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.649 line:0%
but it's dressed up like
a kooky parade float,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:02.087 line:0%
and it goes headlong into some
crazy apocalyptic ending

00:02:02.087 --> 00:02:03.857 line:0%
that I think people will enjoy.

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:05.859 line:0%
-I absolutely enjoyed it
and I loved it.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.661 line:0%
And there's certain things where
I would love to just go like,

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.763 line:0%
"I think that actually might
have happened.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.265 line:0%
Nope. That one definitely
did not happen.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:13.466 line:0%
-It's just a monkey puzzle.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.868 line:0%
-It really is so many
good things in here.

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:16.903 line:0%
-That's right. It's a labyrinth.

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:20.372 line:0%
-It is -- But, I mean, so --

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:23.142 line:0%
It's based sort of --
It's sort of true.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.243 line:0%
-Kind of?
-Yeah.

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:25.477 line:0%
Well, it is true.

00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:27.980 line:0%
All of it is -- None of it
is true, actually --

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.282 line:0%
or all of it is real,
but none of it is true.

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:32.952 line:0%
I don't even know anywhere.
I'm so confused.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:37.623 line:0%
I'm like Donald Trump on a
prompter at this point.

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:38.691 line:0%
-I will --

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.794 line:0%
-No, it's -- It is --
It is madness.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.463 line:0%
It is -- You know,
Jim Carrey in the book

00:02:44.463 --> 00:02:47.366 line:0%
represents me and it
represents all the false ideas

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.302 line:0%
everybody has
ever had about fame.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.303 line:0%
-Yes.

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:52.639 line:0%
-So it's doing a lot of work,

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:54.907 line:0%
and there's a lot
to be had underneath.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.009 line:0%
And you can enjoy it just
on the surface just as

00:02:57.009 --> 00:03:02.448 line:0%
a fun, crazy story of the
magic and madness of Hollywood,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.383 line:0%
but it's deeper than that.

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:06.819 line:0%
The apocalypse goes within
in this one.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.488 line:0%
-I would expect
none less from you,

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.456 line:0%
because you always do something
so memorable and creative

00:03:11.456 --> 00:03:12.725 line:0%
with anything you do.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.627 line:0%
And I go,
"Oh, this is brilliant."

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:17.463 line:0%
'Cause you could have just
put out a book saying,

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.699 line:0%
"Well, I was born
back in Toronto in..."

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:23.469 line:0%
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah, exactly. No.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.438 line:0%
I used to go up to
the Esso station

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:28.540 line:0%
and jump up and down
on the bell and make it ring.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.976 line:0%
Yeah, that kind of stuff.

00:03:30.976 --> 00:03:33.545 line:0%
I was hit with a hockey puck
at 10 years old.

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:37.116 line:0%
Never been the same since.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.952 line:0%
You know? But --
Yeah, no, I wanted to --

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.855 line:0%
You know, Dana and I --
neither of us were interested in

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.658 line:0%
logging the events of my life
in any kind of order.

00:03:46.658 --> 00:03:51.496 line:0%
I think memoirs is
a type of fiction to begin with.

00:03:51.496 --> 00:03:52.631 line:0%
We both felt that way.

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:54.067 line:0%
You know, you read
most people's memoirs,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:58.604 line:0%
you know less about them when
you're done than when you began.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:02.608 line:0%
So, you know, it's kind of
a strange form, anyway,

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.610 line:0%
that gets
altered and exaggerated,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.946 line:0%
and a lot of lying goes on.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.248 line:0%
So we just took
it a little further.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:14.353 line:0%
-Going through this writing
process, did it get emotional?

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:16.289 line:0%
Did it get like, "I don't know
if we can do this"?

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:17.856 line:0%
-Yeah, it's super-emotional.

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:19.792 line:0%
It's super-emotional, especially
when I do anything

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.093 line:0%
about my father, you know?

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:23.695 line:0%
Anything about my dad
will open me up.

00:04:23.695 --> 00:04:26.866 line:0%
You know, it's a matter of time
before, you know,

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:32.438 line:0%
the string gets pulled and
I become a living example of

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:36.008 line:0%
Samuel Barber's
"Adagio for Strings."

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:37.676 line:0%
♪ Ahh, ahh, ahh ♪

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:39.745 line:0%
You know?
-It starts coming.

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:41.648 line:0%
-You go,
"Okay, I need two seconds."

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:42.748 line:0%
-Exactly.

00:04:42.748 --> 00:04:44.283 line:0%
-Was your dad the first person

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:46.285 line:0%
to introduce you
to comedy clubs?

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:47.553 line:0%
-Yeah, he was the guy that said,

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.788 line:0%
"Hey, there's these
places called comedy clubs.

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:52.424 line:0%
And you've got to stop
doing this in your living room."

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:54.393 line:0%
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.728 line:0%
-Would he perform at parties
at your house and stuff?

00:04:56.728 --> 00:04:59.932 line:0%
-Yeah, every time people came
over, I was the show.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:04.403 line:0%
You know, they would be greeted
by an 8-year-old

00:05:04.403 --> 00:05:08.707 line:0%
throwing himself down a huge
flight of stares, at their feet.

00:05:08.707 --> 00:05:09.909 line:0%
And they would be aghast.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:11.443 line:0%
And then I'd get up and say,

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:13.546 line:0%
"Let's see that again,
in slow motion," like that.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:17.482 line:0%
And I'd go back up
and I'd come down...

00:05:17.482 --> 00:05:19.885 line:0%
and do every step
in slow motion.

00:05:19.885 --> 00:05:22.755 line:0%
So I was the show.
I was the show. Yeah.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:23.956 line:0%
-That was my move, too.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.726 line:0%
I would fall down the
stairs with a paper plate

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:29.662 line:0%
and pretend I was Chevy Chase
and say, "Live from New York..."

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.598 line:0%
And then my parents would give
me 50 cents or a dollar

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.800 line:0%
or something to do
Rodney Dangerfield impressions

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.801 line:0%
for the party.

00:05:35.801 --> 00:05:36.835 line:0%
-Oh, nice.

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:39.605 line:0%
-And I know Rodney's
in the book, too.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:42.742 line:0%
-Rodney's in the book.
Rodney's roaring back.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.309 line:0%
-Yeah.
-Roaring back.

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:47.780 line:0%
His essence has been sold,
and he will be seen again.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.049 line:0%
-What was his
first experience with you?

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.951 line:0%
Was it "Young Comedians?" No.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:53.686 line:0%
-No, I wasn't on that.
I wasn't on that.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.054 line:0%
I was touring with him, like,

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:56.856 line:0%
at Caesars Palace
and all these places

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.658 line:0%
where people were just
scratching their heads

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.159 line:0%
'cause I was experimenting.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.794 line:0%
And he would say,
"Man, they're looking at you

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:04.029 line:0%
like you're from another
friggin' planet," you know?

00:06:04.029 --> 00:06:06.766 line:0%
And he just loved my greenness.

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.700 line:0%
He would laugh openly
out of nowhere

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.936 line:0%
and just look at my face
and start laughing.

00:06:10.936 --> 00:06:13.005 line:0%
And he'd ask questions like,
"Hey, kid,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.274 line:0%
have you ever been in love?"

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.042 line:0%
You know? And stuff like that.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:18.511 line:0%
-I love him.
-Yeah.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.879 line:0%
But I got a great e-mail from
Joan Dangerfield.

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:22.482 line:0%
She was so excited
about the book.

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:24.082 line:0%
She read it in two sittings.

00:06:24.082 --> 00:06:26.786 line:0%
And she said just,
"Rodney would be so happy."

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:30.890 line:0%
You know, when he passed away,
she sent me this beautiful box

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.926 line:0%
with his favorite shirt
and his pot pipe,

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:35.461 line:0%
which is --
You know, If you knew him,

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.530 line:0%
that's pretty much the grail.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.831 line:0%
It's the grail.
-Is that right?

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:41.934 line:0%
-Rodney's pot pipe is
the grail, man.

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:44.370 line:0%
-I started flipping out
because, you know,

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.305 line:0%
I read the dedication first.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:49.808 line:0%
And then the first quote in here
is Marshall McLuhan.

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:53.279 line:0%
It said, "For the name of a man
is a numbing blow

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.980 line:0%
from which he never recovers."

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.116 line:0%
And I go, "That's interesting."

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:59.051 line:0%
I'm trying to think of what
that -- Interesting."

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:00.519 line:0%
I didn't even think about that.

00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:03.489 line:0%
And you kind of get into it
in this story about becoming --

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:06.024 line:0%
-Right.
-It gets deep, man.

00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:07.259 line:0%
-The self.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:08.995 line:0%
You know,
the construct of the self,

00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:11.998 line:0%
the thing that you create to

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:14.099 line:0%
negotiate your way
through the world.

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:17.302 line:0%
The face that you leave in a jar
by the door, you know?

00:07:17.302 --> 00:07:19.104 line:0%
I mean, that's really
what it's about.

00:07:19.104 --> 00:07:20.873 line:0%
Your soul doesn't stop
at the edge of your skin.

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:22.875 line:0%
It's one big soul,
and we're all sharing it.

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:26.211 line:0%
-When did you figure that out?

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:28.881 line:0%
-I've been pounded by
the universe several times.

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:32.418 line:0%
So, you know,
every time you get hit,

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:36.822 line:0%
you know, T-boned by life,
you're dragged to the bottom of

00:07:36.822 --> 00:07:38.691 line:0%
the Mariana Trench again.

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:43.095 line:0%
And when you survive that,
sooner or later,

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:46.265 line:0%
you're kind of --
you're an experienced person.

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:48.200 line:0%
And if it doesn't kill you,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:52.471 line:0%
if you don't let it, you know,
make you bitter

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:55.441 line:0%
or disillusioned about life
and you keep your faith,

00:07:55.441 --> 00:08:00.613 line:0%
you eventually become a real
son of a bitch.

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:01.847 line:0%
-Wait, wait. What?

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:03.115 line:0%
-No. I'm kidding.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:06.184 line:0%
No, you eventually --
[ Both laugh ]

00:08:06.184 --> 00:08:08.387 line:0%
You eventually get to
a place where you're realize,

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:10.990 line:0%
"Oh, God, well, that's --"

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.225 line:0%
You start too see things
for what they really are.

00:08:13.225 --> 00:08:16.628 line:0%
This is experience.
It's not who I am.

00:08:16.628 --> 00:08:17.663 line:0%
None of this is who I am.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:20.165 line:0%
And you're stronger from it,

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:22.701 line:0%
you enjoy things more,

00:08:22.701 --> 00:08:23.769 line:0%
and you get glimpses.

00:08:23.769 --> 00:08:25.204 line:0%
I get glimpses
every once in a while

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:27.673 line:0%
of that feeling of freedom --

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.808 line:0%
you know,
actual freedom from self,

00:08:29.808 --> 00:08:31.210 line:0%
freedom from the ideas of you.

